title: Deleting duplicate copies
tags: Duplicates
kind: guide
summary: In the duplicates panel, keep the copy under the cursor and trash the others, or trash one copy — your choice, never a heuristic's.

Which copy survives is always yours to choose. Nothing picks one for you, and no
action spans several groups. In the [[duplicates panel]], both deletions act on
the row under the cursor:

* ''keep this one'' — <<key "duplicates:keep" "p k">>, and the first item of the context menu: trashes every ''other'' copy of the group. It is confirmed, since it touches several files: the confirmation names every path it will trash, and how much space pruning the trash will then reclaim.
* ''trash this one'' — <<key "item:delete" Delete>>: that copy alone, as Delete does anywhere else ([[Deleting an item]]), and like it unconfirmed. On a group row, Delete deletes the group's own metarecord.

The copies go to the [[Trash]], so a mistake is recoverable
([[Restoring from the trash]]); the space is freed when the trash is pruned
([[Trash retention]]).

The group is updated at once — its count, its reclaimable space, and the group
itself, gone once a single copy is left: the trash deletes a copy's metarecord
through the daemon before it moves the bytes, and the daemon re-counts the group
in that same write ([[Duplicate groups]]). The panel then reads the groups again,
keeping the expanded groups and the cursor where they were.

A copy deleted any other way — `mf trash -f`, a file manager — leaves its group
the same way: see [[Duplicate groups]].
